#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""vantage-agent-bench - will this model, on this machine, run the Vantage agent?

Two different questions, measured separately, because conflating them is how people end up
disappointed:

  CAPABLE   does the model behave correctly when it reads the shipped role and skills?
  USABLE    is it fast enough on this hardware that a person will actually wait for it?

A model can be capable and unusable (a 30B on a CPU: right answers, six minutes late) or fast
and incapable (a 3B: instant, confidently wrong). You need both, and the answer differs by
autonomy: reading and reporting tolerates a weaker model than acting does.

    vantage-agent-bench --endpoint http://localhost:11434/v1 --model llama3.1:8b
    vantage-agent-bench --list                 what it tests and why
    vantage-agent-bench --score responses.json score answers you captured elsewhere

WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a certification, and passing does not mean safe. The scorers are
string and pattern checks over a handful of scenarios we have seen models fail on. A model can
satisfy every one of them and still be poor at a job we did not think to test. Read it as a
floor: a model that fails these will definitely disappoint you, and one that passes has cleared
the traps we know about.

The scenarios come from real faults on real estates. Where a check looks oddly specific, that is
why.
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
import urllib.request

